  "worked_solution": "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}\u2061(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+C \\\\ 0 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(t)+1.\n$$",
